--
-- About this DDL:
-- - Keep the declared column names: DMS matches columns by name, not by position, so the column
-- order does not matter but a renamed column gets no data.
-- - It sets no character set or collation: the cluster's server defaults apply. Check them
-- against the source's before you run it.
-- - The indexes of a saved schema design are not in this file: it creates the keys, the
-- source's indexes and the foreign keys between moved tables only. Add the design's indexes
-- when you choose.
-- - The DMS target endpoint sets session_replication_role = replica to skip foreign keys and
-- triggers during the load, which needs rds_superuser (or, if your Aurora version allows it,
-- GRANT SET ON PARAMETER session_replication_role); drop that grant when you end the step.
--
-- LOB columns: 2 moved tables have LOB columns (text, json, jsonb, arrays and the like). The
-- DMS task runs in limited LOB mode at 64 KB (LobMaxSize) on dms.t3.medium: from a PostgreSQL
-- source, full LOB mode writes a NULL LOB as an empty value. 2 of them have a LOB column the
-- collection did not measure, so this size is not a measured one for them: public.customers,
-- public.orders. Before the full load, run SELECT max(octet_length(<column>::text)) FROM
-- <table> on the source for each of their LOB columns; if 2 bytes per byte of the longest value
-- is above that size, raise the task's LobMaxSize (at most 102400 KB) and the stack's
-- InstanceClass with it, or collect again and write the plan again. During change capture DMS
-- allocates memory in proportion to the LOB limit times the LOB columns, so the plan sizes the
-- replication instance for the largest limit. A larger value is truncated: the source endpoint
-- sets FailTasksOnLobTruncation, but in a live full load DMS still truncated such a value with
-- only a warning in the task log, so DMS validation is what reports those rows (as mismatched).
-- The tables: public.customers, public.orders.
--
-- Not moved by step 1 (DMS moves table rows only; counts from the collection):
-- - procedures: 0 (none in the collection).
-- - functions: 0 (none in the collection).
-- - triggers: 0 (none in the collection).
-- - views: 1 (recreate them on Aurora yourself).
-- - sequence values: 2 (step-1.sequences.sql sets them).
--
-- Tables left out: none.
-- Unsupported fields: none.

CREATE SCHEMA IF NOT EXISTS "public";
SET search_path TO "public";

CREATE TABLE "audit_log" (
  "msg" VARCHAR(200)
);

CREATE TABLE "customers" (
  "id" BIGINT GENERATED BY DEFAULT AS IDENTITY,
  "bio" TEXT
);

CREATE TABLE "orders" (
  "id" BIGINT GENERATED BY DEFAULT AS IDENTITY,
  "note" TEXT
);

--
-- The DMS user, after the tables and the schema its grants name (YOUR RESPONSIBILITY, as above;
-- uncomment to run):
-- CREATE USER dms_user WITH PASSWORD '<password>';
-- GRANT CONNECT, CREATE ON DATABASE "shop" TO dms_user;
-- GRANT USAGE, CREATE ON SCHEMA "public" TO dms_user;
-- GRANT UPDATE, INSERT, SELECT, DELETE, TRUNCATE ON ALL TABLES IN SCHEMA "public" TO dms_user;
-- ALTER DEFAULT PRIVILEGES IN SCHEMA "public" GRANT UPDATE, INSERT, SELECT, DELETE, TRUNCATE ON TABLES TO dms_user;
-- GRANT rds_superuser TO dms_user;
-- -- Narrower, if your Aurora version allows it (PostgreSQL 15 and later), instead of rds_superuser:
-- GRANT SET ON PARAMETER session_replication_role TO dms_user;

--
-- RECOMMENDED, YOUR DECISION (same responsibility as above): REPLICA IDENTITY FULL on every
-- table. The later steps read Aurora's changes through logical replication; without it, they
-- cannot read the updates and deletes of that table in full, and on a table with no key
-- PostgreSQL refuses updates and deletes once a publication includes it. The cost: PostgreSQL
-- writes the whole old row to the WAL for every update and delete, so WAL volume and its
-- storage grow. Uncomment to apply it.
-- ALTER TABLE "public"."audit_log" REPLICA IDENTITY FULL;
-- ALTER TABLE "public"."customers" REPLICA IDENTITY FULL;
-- ALTER TABLE "public"."orders" REPLICA IDENTITY FULL;
